<SectionBlock
  id="difference"
  eyebrow="How this differs"
  title="The frameworks already ask for a way to stop a system. These drafts say who holds it and how fast it works."
  description="They are written to sit beside the EU AI Act, the NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001, and the OECD principles, and to make three things checkable: who can stop a running system, how fast, and who carries the cost while it runs."
  variant="default"
>
  <p>
    Each of those frameworks asks for oversight and a means of intervention.
    Each leaves the operator to decide who exercises it, how fast, and what
    record shows that it worked. The drafts here fill in those parameters. They
    also bind any system that decides about people, so they would have applied
    to Post Office Horizon, which was accounting software, not AI.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a system cuts its own workload, the work it drops lands on someone: the
    nurse fixing a scheduler's mistakes, the claimant proving a denial wrong.
    That work is part of the system, so the person doing it should have a say in
    fixing it. The cost should fall on the institution that runs the system, not
    on the people it serves.
  </p>
  <FrameworkComparisonTable />
  <p class="muted small">
    Each item on the right is a property of a running system, so a document
    cannot show it on its own. The drafts have no force until someone adopts
    them. See the
    <a href="/standards#comparison">full standards comparison</a>.
  </p>
</SectionBlock>
